  <subsection title="Simplify Expressions Using the Power Property for Exponents">
    <p>Now let’s look at an exponential expression that contains a power raised to a power. See if you can discover a general property.</p>
    <table>
      <tbody>
        <row>
          <cell />
          <cell />
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell>What does this mean?How many factors altogether?</cell>
          <cell />
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell>So we have</cell>
          <cell />
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell>Notice that 6 is the product of the exponents, 2 and 3.</cell>
          <cell />
        </row>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>We write:</p>
    <equation>$$\begin{matrix} \left( x^{2} \right)^{3} \\ x^{2 \cdot 3} \\ x^{6} \end{matrix}$$</equation>
    <p>We multiplied the exponents. This leads to the Power Property for Exponents.</p>
    <note type="Power Property for Exponents">
      <p>If $$a$$ is a real number, and $$m\ \text{and}\ n$$ are whole numbers, then</p>
      <equation>$${(a^{m})}^{n} = a^{m \cdot n}$$</equation>
      <p>To raise a power to a power, multiply the exponents.</p>
    </note>
    <p>An example with numbers helps to verify this property.</p>
    <equation>$$\begin{array}{rll} \left( 3^{2} \right)^{3} &amp; \overset{?}{=} &amp; 3^{2 \cdot 3} \\ (9)^{3} &amp; \overset{?}{=} &amp; 3^{6} \\ 729 &amp; = &amp; {729\ \text{✓}} \end{array}$$</equation>
    <example number="6.22">
      <problem>
        <p>Simplify: ⓐ $${(y^{5})}^{9}$$ ⓑ $${(4^{4})}^{7}.$$</p>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <p>ⓐ</p>
        <table>
          <tbody>
            <row>
              <cell />
              <cell />
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Use the power property, (am)n = am·n.</cell>
              <cell />
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Simplify.</cell>
              <cell />
            </row>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <p>ⓑ</p>
        <table>
          <tbody>
            <row>
              <cell />
              <cell />
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Use the power property.</cell>
              <cell />
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Simplify.</cell>
              <cell />
            </row>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </solution>
    </example>
    <note type="Try It" number="6.43">
      <p>Simplify: ⓐ $${(b^{7})}^{5}$$ ⓑ $${(5^{4})}^{3}.$$</p>
    </note>
    <note type="Try It" number="6.44">
      <p>Simplify: ⓐ $${(z^{6})}^{9}$$ ⓑ $${(3^{7})}^{7}.$$</p>
    </note>
  </subsection>
  <subsection title="Simplify Expressions Using the Product to a Power Property">
    <p>We will now look at an expression containing a product that is raised to a power. Can you find this pattern?</p>
    <table summary="This table demonstrates the step-by-step expansion and simplification of the algebraic expression (2x) to 2 x .">
      <tbody>
        <row>
          <cell />
          <cell>$$\left( {2x} \right)^{3}$$</cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell>What does this mean?</cell>
          <cell>$$2x \cdot 2x \cdot 2x$$</cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell>We group the like factors together.</cell>
          <cell>$$2 \cdot 2 \cdot 2 \cdot x \cdot x \cdot x$$</cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell>How many factors of 2 and of $$x$$?</cell>
          <cell>$$2^{3} \cdot x^{3}$$</cell>
        </row>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>Notice that each factor was raised to the power and $$\left( {2x} \right)^{3}$$ is $$2^{3} \cdot x^{3}$$.</p>
    <table summary="Illustrates the power of a product rule, showing (2x)^3 expanded as 2^3 * x^3.">
      <tbody>
        <row>
          <cell>We write:</cell>
          <cell>$$\left( {2x} \right)^{3}$$</cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell />
          <cell>$$2^{3} \cdot x^{3}$$</cell>
        </row>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>The exponent applies to each of the factors! This leads to the Product to a Power Property for Exponents.</p>
    <note type="Product to a Power Property for Exponents">
      <p>If $$a$$ and $$b$$ are real numbers and $$m$$ is a whole number, then</p>
      <equation>$${(ab)}^{m} = a^{m}b^{m}$$</equation>
      <p>To raise a product to a power, raise each factor to that power.</p>
    </note>
    <p>An example with numbers helps to verify this property:</p>
    <equation>$$\begin{array}{rll} \left( {2 \cdot 3} \right)^{2} &amp; \overset{?}{=} &amp; {2^{2} \cdot 3^{2}} \\ 6^{2} &amp; \overset{?}{=} &amp; {4 \cdot 9} \\ 36 &amp; = &amp; {36\ \text{✓}} \end{array}$$</equation>
    <example number="6.23">
      <problem>
        <p>Simplify: ⓐ $${(-9d)}^{2}$$ ⓑ $${(3mn)}^{3}.$$</p>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <list type="ol" style="1">
          <item>
            <table>
              <tbody>
                <row>
                  <cell />
                  <cell />
                </row>
                <row>
                  <cell>Use Power of a Product Property, (ab)m = ambm.</cell>
                  <cell />
                </row>
                <row>
                  <cell>Simplify.</cell>
                  <cell />
                </row>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </item>
          <item>
            <table>
              <tbody>
                <row>
                  <cell />
                  <cell />
                </row>
                <row>
                  <cell>Use Power of a Product Property, (ab)m = ambm.</cell>
                  <cell />
                </row>
                <row>
                  <cell>Simplify.</cell>
                  <cell />
                </row>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </item>
        </list>
      </solution>
    </example>
    <note type="Try It" number="6.45">
      <p>Simplify: ⓐ $${(-12y)}^{2}$$ ⓑ $${(2wx)}^{5}.$$</p>
    </note>
    <note type="Try It" number="6.46">
      <p>Simplify: ⓐ $${(5wx)}^{3}$$ ⓑ $${(-3y)}^{3}.$$</p>
    </note>
  </subsection>
  <subsection title="Simplify Expressions by Applying Several Properties">
    <p>We now have three properties for multiplying expressions with exponents. Let’s summarize them and then we’ll do some examples that use more than one of the properties.</p>
    <note type="Properties of Exponents">
      <p>If $$a\ \text{and}\ b$$ are real numbers, and $$m\ \text{and}\ n$$ are whole numbers, then</p>
      <table summary="Common exponent properties and their corresponding mathematical formulas.">
        <tbody>
          <row>
            <cell>Product Property</cell>
            <cell>$$\begin{array}{rll} {a^{m} \cdot a^{n}} &amp; = &amp; a^{m + n} \end{array}$$</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>Power Property</cell>
            <cell>$$\begin{array}{rll} {(a^{m})}^{n} &amp; = &amp; a^{m \cdot n} \end{array}$$</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>Product to a Power</cell>
            <cell>$$\begin{array}{rll} {(ab)}^{m} &amp; = &amp; {a^{m}b^{m}} \end{array}$$</cell>
          </row>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </note>
    <p>All exponent properties hold true for any real numbers $$m\ \text{and}\ n$$. Right now, we only use whole number exponents.</p>
    <example number="6.24">
      <problem>
        <p>Simplify: ⓐ $${(y^{3})}^{6}{(y^{5})}^{4}$$ ⓑ $$\left( {-6x^{4}y^{5}} \right)^{2}.$$</p>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <table summary="Steps demonstrating the simplification of algebraic expressions with exponents using various exponent properties.">
          <tbody>
            <row>
              <cell>ⓐ</cell>
              <cell>$${(y^{3})}^{6}{(y^{5})}^{4}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Use the Power Property.</cell>
              <cell>$$y^{18} \cdot y^{20}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Add the exponents.</cell>
              <cell>$$y^{38}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>ⓑ</cell>
              <cell>$$\left( {-6x^{4}y^{5}} \right)^{2}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Use the Product to a Power Property.</cell>
              <cell>$${(-6)}^{2}{(x^{4})}^{2}{(y^{5})}^{2}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Use the Power Property.</cell>
              <cell>$${(-6)}^{2}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Simplify.</cell>
              <cell>$$36x^{8}y^{10}$$</cell>
            </row>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </solution>
    </example>
    <note type="Try It" number="6.47">
      <p>Simplify: ⓐ $${(a^{4})}^{5}{(a^{7})}^{4}$$ ⓑ $$\left( {-2c^{4}d^{2}} \right)^{3}.$$</p>
    </note>
    <note type="Try It" number="6.48">
      <p>Simplify: ⓐ $$\left( {-3x^{6}y^{7}} \right)^{4}$$ ⓑ $${(q^{4})}^{5}{(q^{3})}^{3}.$$</p>
    </note>
    <example number="6.25">
      <problem>
        <p>Simplify: ⓐ $${(5m)}^{2}(3m^{3})$$ ⓑ $${(3x^{2}y)}^{4}{(2xy^{2})}^{3}.$$</p>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <table summary="Step-by-step simplification of two algebraic expressions involving exponents and their properties.">
          <tbody>
            <row>
              <cell>ⓐ</cell>
              <cell>$${(5m)}^{2}(3m^{3})$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Raise $$5m$$ to the second power.</cell>
              <cell>$$5^{2}m^{2} \cdot 3m^{3}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Simplify.</cell>
              <cell>$$25m^{2} \cdot 3m^{3}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Use the Commutative Property.</cell>
              <cell>$$25 \cdot 3 \cdot m^{2} \cdot m^{3}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Multiply the constants and add the exponents.</cell>
              <cell>$$75m^{5}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>ⓑ</cell>
              <cell>$${(3x^{2}y)}^{4}{(2xy^{2})}^{3}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Use the Product to a Power Property.</cell>
              <cell>$$(3^{4}x^{8}y^{4})(2^{3}x^{3}y^{6})$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Simplify.</cell>
              <cell>$$(81x^{8}y^{4})(8x^{3}y^{6})$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Use the Commutative Property.</cell>
              <cell>$$81 \cdot 8 \cdot x^{8} \cdot x^{3} \cdot y^{4} \cdot y^{6}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Multiply the constants and add the exponents.</cell>
              <cell>$$648x^{11}y^{10}$$</cell>
            </row>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </solution>
    </example>
    <note type="Try It" number="6.49">
      <p>Simplify: ⓐ $${(5n)}^{2}(3n^{10})$$ ⓑ $${(c^{4}d^{2})}^{5}{(3cd^{5})}^{4}.$$</p>
    </note>
    <note type="Try It" number="6.50">
      <p>Simplify: ⓐ $${(a^{3}b^{2})}^{6}{(4ab^{3})}^{4}$$ ⓑ $${(2x)}^{3}(5x^{7}).$$</p>
    </note>
  </subsection>
  <subsection title="Multiply Monomials">
    <p>Since a monomial is an algebraic expression, we can use the properties of exponents to multiply monomials.</p>
    <example number="6.26">
      <problem>
        <p>Multiply: $$\left( {3x^{2}} \right)\left( {-4x^{3}} \right).$$</p>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <table summary="Step-by-step solution for multiplying monomial expressions using the Commutative Property.">
          <tbody>
            <row>
              <cell />
              <cell>$$\left( {3x^{2}} \right)\left( {-4x^{3}} \right)$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Use the Commutative Property to rearrange the terms.</cell>
              <cell>$$3 \cdot (-4) \cdot x^{2} \cdot x^{3}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Multiply.</cell>
              <cell>$$-12x^{5}$$</cell>
            </row>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </solution>
    </example>
    <note type="Try It" number="6.51">
